      <abstract>
        <sec><title>Background</title><p>Cholera causes rapid dehydration through watery diarrhoea.</p></sec>
        <sec><title>Results</title><p>Low-osmolarity oral rehydration solution reduced stool output compared with standard solution.</p></sec>
      </abstract>
    </article-meta>
  </front>
  <body>
    <sec id="s1">
      <title>Introduction</title>
      <p>Dehydration is the main cause of death in cholera <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="r1">[1]</xref>. Early rehydration saves lives.</p>
    </sec>
    <sec id="s2">
      <title>Methods</title>
      <sec id="s2a">
        <title>Participants</title>
        <p>Adults with acute watery diarrhoea were enrolled at two synthetic sites.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item><p>Age 18 to 65 years</p></list-item>
          <list-item><p>Stool culture positive for Vibrio cholerae</p></list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
    </sec>
    <sec id="s3">
      <title>Results</title>
      <p>Stool output fell faster with low-osmolarity solution (<xref ref-type="fig" rid="f1">Figure 1</xref>; <xref ref-type="table" rid="t1">Table 1</xref>).
        <fig id="f1">
          <label>Figure 1</label>
          <caption><p>Cumulative stool output over 48 hours in the two treatment groups.</p></caption>
          <graphic xlink:href="synthetic-g001"/>
        </fig>
      </p>
      <table-wrap id="t1">
        <label>Table 1</label>
        <caption><p>Primary outcomes by treatment group.</p></caption>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Outcome</th><th>Low-osmolarity ORS</th><th>Standard ORS</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Stool output (ml/kg)</td><td>180</td><td>240</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Need for IV fluids</td><td colspan="2">Not significantly different</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <table-wrap-foot><p>ORS, oral rehydration solution.</p></table-wrap-foot>
      </table-wrap>
    </sec>
    <sec id="s4">
      <title>Conclusions</title>
      <p>Low-osmolarity oral rehydration solution is effective for adults with cholera.</p>
    </sec>
